<h2>Why a German feed buyer looks at India at all</h2>
<p>This is worth addressing directly, because it's not obvious from price alone. Indian soybean meal typically costs more than Brazilian, Argentine or US meal — Indian yields are lower and domestic crush demand for oil is strong, which keeps export prices elevated. So why does Germany, along with France, consistently rank among India's top soybean meal destinations?</p>
<p>The answer is non-GMO status. India doesn't permit commercial cultivation of genetically modified soybean, so Indian soybean meal is conventional by origin — not through a certification scheme, but structurally, because the crop grown in India isn't GM in the first place. For German and broader European feed programmes serving non-GMO-labelled meat, dairy and poultry supply chains, that's a characteristic price competition from South America can't replicate.</p>

<h2>Specification: get the protein grade right</h2>
<p>46% and 48% minimum crude protein are the two standard export grades, solvent extracted. German buyers, in our experience, specify tightly and expect a proper certificate of analysis — moisture, crude fibre, crude fat, sand/silica content and urease activity, which indicates whether heat treatment during processing was correctly calibrated. Have your specification precise before requesting a quote; vague specs slow the process down on this market more than most.</p>

<h2>Documentation for German/EU import</h2>
<p>As an EU member, Germany's import requirements run through EU food law:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Phytosanitary certificate, registered via the EU's TRACES platform</li>
  <li>Non-GMO declaration, supporting your buyer's own labelling claims downstream</li>
  <li>Certificate of analysis covering the full specification panel</li>
</ul>
<p>German importers are widely known — even within the EU's shared regulatory framework — for particularly rigorous documentation and traceability expectations. We'd recommend preparing this documentation proactively rather than waiting to be asked.</p>

<h2>Packing and logistics</h2>
<p>Standard packing is 25kg, 40kg or 50kg PP bags, or 1 MT jumbo bags (FIBC) for buyers with bulk intake handling — around 24-26 MT per 20ft container, loading being weight-limited rather than volume-limited. Transit to Hamburg runs 18-24 days.</p>

<h2>Market context</h2>
<p>Germany appeared among India's top five soybean meal destinations by volume in the most recent marketing year reported by the Soybean Processors Association of India (SOPA), alongside France, Nepal, Bangladesh and Kenya, which together accounted for roughly 55% of total export volume. The source didn't break out individual per-country figures, so we're reporting the ranking rather than a number we can't verify — consistent with how we handle every data point on this site.</p>

<h2>What to expect on a first order</h2>
<p>German buyers new to sourcing soybean meal from India should expect the specification and documentation conversation to take somewhat longer than with less rigorous markets — not because anything is wrong, but because German feed buyers typically want the full certificate of analysis panel confirmed before committing, plus clarity on non-GMO documentation chain of custody. Build this into your first-order timeline expectations, and treat it as a one-time setup cost — once specification and documentation are established, repeat orders move considerably faster.</p>
